---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { GITHUB_URL } from "../consts";
import { PRESET_ROSTERS } from "../data/rosters";

const SETUP_PROMPT = "Set up rostr for me by following the instructions at https://getrostr.sh";

const ROSTER_MEMBERS = [
  { emoji: "🧙", name: "Gandalf", task: "Fix auth redirect loop", where: "rostr/api", since: "12m" },
  { emoji: "🌱", name: "Samwise", task: "Migrate billing to Stripe v3", where: "smart-notes", since: "1h" },
  { emoji: "💍", name: "Frodo", task: "Flaky e2e tests on CI", where: "rostr/site", since: "3m" },
  { emoji: "👑", name: "Aragorn", task: "Postgres index audit", where: "cloud", since: "40m" },
];

const IDLE_MEMBERS = ["Legolas", "Gimli", "Boromir", "Pippin", "Merry", "Balrog"];

const COMMANDS = [
  { command: "rostr create <roster>", description: "Create a roster from a preset or your own names. Choose --local or --cloud." },
  { command: "rostr presets", description: "See all the pre-built rosters you can start from." },
  { command: "rostr use <roster>", description: "Use a roster for the current project. Projects without one use your default roster." },
  { command: "rostr draft", description: "Draft the next available agent, A team first, and optionally say what it's working on with --task. Your hooks run this at the start of every session." },
  { command: "rostr assign <name> <description>", description: "Assign an agent its work, or reassign it when the work changes. This is what rostr list shows." },
  { command: "rostr list", description: "See who's on your roster and what each agent is working on." },
  { command: "rostr release <name>", description: "Send an agent back to the bench when its work is done." },
  { command: "rostr edit", description: "Add, remove, or rename members, pick your A team, or turn emoji on and off." },
  { command: "rostr transfer <roster>", description: "Move a local roster to the cloud, so cloud sessions can draft from it too." },
  { command: "rostr disband", description: "Delete your roster." },
];

---

<Layout
  title="rostr: give your agents names"
  description="rostr gives each of your coding agents a name, so you can remember who's doing what. Works with Claude Code and Codex."
>
    <!-- Hero -->
    <section class="hero">
      <h1>Give your agents names and remember who's doing what.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        rostr drafts a name for every coding agent session you start, from a roster you pick. Gandalf is on the auth
        bug, Samwise is migrating billing, and you never have to dig through five tabs called "New session" again.
      </p>
      <div class="cta">
        <button class="copy" data-copy={SETUP_PROMPT}>
          <span class="copy-text">“{SETUP_PROMPT}”</span>
          <span class="copy-label">Copy</span>
        </button>
        <a class="button-secondary" href={GITHUB_URL}>View on GitHub</a>
      </div>
      <p class="works-with">
        Works out of the box with <strong>Claude Code</strong> and <strong>Codex</strong>, in local and cloud sessions.
      </p>

      <div class="terminal" aria-label="Example output of rostr list">
        <div class="terminal-bar"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
        <pre><span class="prompt">$</span> rostr list
<span class="dim">fellowship · A team: 4 of 6 drafted</span>
{ROSTER_MEMBERS.map((member) => `\n${member.emoji} ${member.name.padEnd(9)} ${member.task.padEnd(30)} ${member.where.padEnd(12)} ${member.since}`).join("")}

<span class="dim">idle: {IDLE_MEMBERS.join(", ")}</span>
</pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Why -->
    <section class="section narrow">
      <h2>Think like an engineering manager</h2>
      <p>
        Running five agents at once is easy. Remembering what each one is doing is not. Every workstream you have in
        flight is something you're holding in your head, and anonymous sessions make it harder: "Untitled 4" and "Fix
        bug" tell you nothing when you come back an hour later.
      </p>
      <p>
        A good engineering manager doesn't keep a mental list of tickets. They know their team, and they know who's on
        what. Ask who's handling the billing migration and the answer is instant. rostr gives your agents that kind of
        identity, so you're checking in with Samwise about billing instead of hunting for the right terminal.
      </p>
      <p>
        It works outside the terminal too. When an agent reaches out to you on Slack, in a PR comment, or anywhere else,
        it signs with its name: "Gandalf here, the auth fix is ready for review." You know exactly who's asking, and
        getting back to that session is as easy as finding Gandalf in your session list.
      </p>
      <p>
        Names from stories you already know work best. Samwise comes with a personality attached, so "Samwise is on
        billing" sticks in a way that "session 3" never will.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- How it works -->
    <section class="section" id="how-it-works">
      <h2>How it works</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">rostr is a small CLI plus a skill that teaches your agent how to use it.</p>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <h3>Pick a roster</h3>
          <p>Run <code>rostr create</code> and choose one of the pre-built rosters, or bring your own list of names.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Every session gets drafted</h3>
          <p>
            When a session starts, a hook drafts the next available name and tells the agent who it is. The name goes
            at the front of the session title: <code>🧙 Gandalf · Fix auth redirect loop</code>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Agents go back to the bench</h3>
          <p>
            The rostr skill has your agent release its name when the work is done, so the same familiar faces keep
            coming back and your roster stays small.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <!-- TODO: screenshot of named sessions in the Claude Code / Codex sidebar -->
      <figure class="screenshot-placeholder">
        <span>Screenshot: named sessions in the Claude Code sidebar</span>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <!-- Local and cloud -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Works locally and in the cloud</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">
        rostr names your agents wherever they run: in a terminal on your laptop, or in a cloud session like Claude Code
        on the web or Codex cloud.
      </p>
      <div class="cards">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>💻 Local sessions</h3>
          <p>
            Roster state lives on disk on your machine. Drafting is instant, there's no account to make, and nothing
            goes over the network.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>☁️ Cloud sessions</h3>
          <p>
            Cloud sessions don't share a disk, so they draft from the rostr API at <code>api.getrostr.sh</code>
            instead. Every cloud agent gets its name from the same roster, and no two end up with the same one.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Commands -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Commands</h2>
      <dl class="commands">
        {COMMANDS.map((item) => (
          <div class="command-row">
            <dt><code>{item.command}</code></dt>
            <dd>{item.description}</dd>
          </div>
        ))}
      </dl>
      <p class="docs-link"><a href="/cli">Read the full CLI docs →</a></p>
    </section>

    <!-- Rosters -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Squad up</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">
        rostr comes with fun pre-built rosters, or you can make your own from any list of names. Hover over a roster to
        meet its A team and B team. Every member gets an emoji; if that's not your thing, turn them off with
        <code>rostr edit</code>.
      </p>
      <ul class="rosters">
        {PRESET_ROSTERS.map((roster) => (
          <li tabindex="0">
            <span class="roster-emoji">{roster.emoji}</span>
            <span>
              <strong>{roster.name}</strong>
              <small>{roster.source}</small>
            </span>
            <div class="roster-teams" role="tooltip">
              <b>A team</b>
              <div class="chips">
                {roster.aTeam.map((member, index) => <span class="chip chip-a" style={`--i: ${index}`}>{member}</span>)}
              </div>
              <b>B team</b>
              <div class="chips">
                {roster.bTeam.map((member, index) => <span class="chip" style={`--i: ${index + 6}`}>{member}</span>)}
              </div>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))}
        <li class="roster-custom" tabindex="0">
          <span class="roster-emoji">✏️</span>
          <span>
            <strong>Your own</strong>
            <small>Any list of names</small>
          </span>
          <div class="roster-teams" role="tooltip">
            <b>A team</b>
            <div class="chips"><span class="chip chip-a" style="--i: 0">⭐ Your six favorites</span></div>
            <b>B team</b>
            <div class="chips"><span class="chip" style="--i: 1">🪑 Everyone else</span></div>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <div class="cards team-cards">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>🅰️ A team, B team</h3>
          <p>
            Twenty names are too many to get to know, so every roster has a small A team: six by default, picked with
            <code>rostr edit</code>. rostr always drafts from the A team first and only calls up the B team when all your
            starters are busy.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>📁 A roster per project</h3>
          <p>
            Make as many rosters as you like and give each project its own with <code>rostr use</code>. The Fellowship
            works on rostr, the Smash Bros work on your app, and a name tells you the project as well as the task.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Install -->
    <section class="section" id="install">
      <h2>Install</h2>
      <div class="cards">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Ask your agent</h3>
          <p>Point Claude Code or Codex at this page and it will install rostr, set up the hooks and skill, and help you pick a roster.</p>
          <button class="copy copy-block" data-copy={SETUP_PROMPT}>
            <span class="copy-text">“{SETUP_PROMPT}”</span>
            <span class="copy-label">Copy</span>
          </button>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Do it yourself</h3>
          <p>Install with Homebrew, create a roster, then install the hooks and skill for your agents.</p>
          <pre class="code"><span class="prompt">$</span> brew install piazzatron/tap/rostr
<span class="prompt">$</span> rostr create work --preset fellowship
<span class="prompt">$</span> rostr setup <span class="dim"># hooks + skill</span></pre>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

  <script>
    document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-copy]").forEach((button) => {
      button.addEventListener("click", async () => {
        await navigator.clipboard.writeText(button.dataset.copy ?? "");
        const label = button.querySelector(".copy-label");
        if (!label) return;
        label.textContent = "Copied";
        setTimeout(() => (label.textContent = "Copy"), 1500);
      });
    });

    // Keep each roster's A team / B team popover on screen by nudging it left when it would overflow
    document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(".rosters li").forEach((tile) => {
      const popover = tile.querySelector<HTMLElement>(".roster-teams");
      if (!popover) return;
      // Measured a frame later, once the hover style has made the popover visible. Layout sizes (offsetLeft and
      // offsetWidth) ignore the pop-in animation's scale and rotation.
      const keepPopoverOnScreen = () =>
        requestAnimationFrame(() => {
          popover.style.marginLeft = "0px";
          const popoverRight = tile.getBoundingClientRect().left + popover.offsetLeft + popover.offsetWidth;
          const overflow = popoverRight - (document.documentElement.clientWidth - 16);
          if (overflow > 0) popover.style.marginLeft = `${-overflow}px`;
        });
      tile.addEventListener("pointerenter", keepPopoverOnScreen);
      tile.addEventListener("focusin", keepPopoverOnScreen);
    });
  </script>
</Layout>
